  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - The current algorithm struggles to consistently improve upon the record 0.38085 solution primarily because it overlooks multi-modal patterns inherent in step function optimization. Its hill climbing and gradient-based methods often get trapped in local minima. The key insight is that optimal solutions frequently exhibit periodic structure across multiple scales.\n   - This approach shifts from local search to multi-scale global optimization, using aperiodic and periodic patterns derived from principal eigenvectors. It replaces hill climbing with a spectral-distance based \"tracker\" that maintains multiple promising solutions across scales, enabling escape from local optima.\n   - Limitations: This increases memory usage and requires careful budget allocation across scales. But expected gains in solution quality may justify this.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Objective Function f(h): Minimize max_{k} \u222bh(x)\u00b2 dx + \u222b(1-h(x+k))\u00b2 dx (proxy for Erd\u0151s overlap)\n   - Linear constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - Step function representation: h(x) = sum_{i} h[i] * rect(x - x[i]), but we work via samples\n   - Periodic embedding: Find h with shorter-period repetition structure\n   - Solution tracing: Maintain population of (h, scale) pairs with spectral energy matching record solutions\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   a. Input: h_parent (n=180) if available, else starting point\n   b. Phase 1: Spectral Initialization (if parent available)\n      - Compute top eigenvalues of rounded Toeplitz matrices from samples\n      - Create aperiodic and periodic patterns around eigenvalues\n   c. Phase 2: Multi-scale Tracking\n      - For each trial scale s from 1 to 3:\n        * Slice original grid into s segments\n        * Solve local 1D eigenvalue problems on each segment\n        * Project parent's eigenvectors into s-segment space\n        * Use greedy hill climbing with additive repair\n      - Evaluate periodic solutions with seamless exist to optimization\n   d. Phase 3: Global Optimization\n      - Track multiple solution trajectories with decreasing scale factor\n      - Merge intermediate results with parent using alignment metrics\n      - For each trajectory, optimize via gradient-free constrained methods\n      - Return best solution meeting Erd\u0151s evaluation criteria\n   e. Forced Restart: If time allowing, run periodic solution optimization with different constant embeddings\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Default hyperparameters: \u03f5=0.1, scale range [0.7,0.3]\n   - Initial population: Aperiodic if parent unavailable (uniform mixing), periodic if available (local eigenvector subspaces)\n   - Adaptive tuning: Annealing schedule based on largest scale factor achieved\n   - Backup variations: Spectral embedding with Fourier coefficients, probabilistic restart schedule\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Time allocation: 60% per-scale optimization, 30% repair/restart\n   - Memory: Track up to 5 active solution trajectories simultaneously\n   - Monitoring: Check optimization boundaries and enforce domain feasibility bounds at each repair step\n   - Deadline robustness: Graceful exit on best improvement after 4 restarts without progress\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Verify sum(h)==180/2 within 2e-7 tolerance\n   - Compute final C5 using exact correlation (must match parent's evaluation method)\n   - Include monitoring for NaN/inf candidates\n   - Return (h_listed, c5_final, n_points)",
